{%- liquid
  assign price_target = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign price = price_target.price
  assign compare_at_price = price_target.compare_at_price
-%}
<div class="price{% if compare_at_price > price %} price--sale{% endif %}" data-price>
  {%- if compare_at_price > price -%}
    <span class="visually-hidden">Regulärer Preis</span>
    <s>{{ compare_at_price | money }}</s>
    <span class="visually-hidden">Aktionspreis</span>
  {%- endif -%}
  <strong>{{ price | money }}</strong>
  {%- if price_target.unit_price_measurement -%}
    <small>{{ price_target.unit_price | money }} / {{ price_target.unit_price_measurement.reference_value }}{{ price_target.unit_price_measurement.reference_unit }}</small>
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Every variant's price, already formatted by the shop's own money format.

    Switching variant used to leave this number alone: the picker swapped the
    id behind the buy button and the figure above it stayed where it started.
    That was survivable while variants only differed by size. It is not
    survivable with a variant that costs four euro more — the surcharge would
    never appear until the cart.

    Formatted here rather than in script because the money format is a shop
    setting (currency, separators, whether tax is shown), and a second
    implementation of it in JavaScript is a second thing to get wrong.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if product.variants.size > 1 -%}
    <script type="application/json" data-variant-prices>{ {%- for variant in product.variants -%}"{{ variant.id }}": {{ variant.price | money | json }}{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}{%- endfor -%} }</script>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
